

Phase 2 is complete. The headline results:

What the embeddings showed
DNABERT-2 sees SNPs in short slices. Embedding cosine distance tracks true sequence identity at Spearman −0.80 across all 2,328 pairs (per-region −0.65 to −0.89). The two anthracis V6 haplotypes, one SNP apart, are clearly separated in embedding space — the validity check the gLM arm needed.

The arms agree on region rankings (Spearman 0.73 between identity-based divergence and embedding-based divergence), and V6 separability is at chance (LOO accuracy 0.528), confirming the expected-null classification of B. anthracis — its silhouette is negative in every region; it sits inside the cereus-group cloud everywhere.

V1 and V4 carry the most partial signal (LOO ~0.80), but exact string identity between at least one anthracis–cereus pair in every region means no region supports a perfect assay.

The bonus finding
The gLM arm's identical-sequence sanity check caught a real bug in the Phase 0–1 identity function — it counted aligned positions rather than matching positions, inflating identities to 1.0 for any equal-length gapless pair. I fixed it, recomputed everything, and added an erratum to the Phase 0–1 report. The conclusions survive (btwn_max = 1.0 is now a genuine string-identity claim), and one finding actually strengthens: V6 carries the largest mean between-group divergence of any region — directionally consistent with Chakravorty's heterogeneity claim, while still failing to separate the groups.

That the gLM arm validated the baseline arm is itself a protocol argument: keep both arms, and make the identical-sequence check a standing QA step.

Practical notes
Running DNABERT-2 on this CPU-only sandbox required three compatibility patches to its custom code (transformers 5.x / torch 2.15 / CPU attention fallback), all documented in the report — no numerical changes to the model. Everything, including the 387 slice embeddings and the corrected pair-level CSV, is in the refreshed bundle.

Recommended next step
The discriminating test must come from a target where partial separation exists: S. aureus vs CoNS (published V1 claim) and B. longum subsp. infantis vs longum — the infant-gut target that ties to your review. I'd also add the k-mer RF baseline and a GC/length covariate check before Phase 3. Want me to run the S. aureus panel next?


