  Three concrete fixes I'd make before Phase 0:
1. Elevate the held-out-taxon transfer test (currently buried in §10 Known Pitfalls) to a headline, pre-registered hypothesis. Whether embeddings generalize to a taxon absent from pretraining is the actual question
anyone adopting regioneer cares about — everything else is a sanity check.
2. Check per-group sequence counts in SILVA/LTP before committing to Panel B, especially the B. longum subspecies split — type-strain-level clades can have single-digit sequence counts, which makes silhouette width
and leave-one-out NN accuracy noisy on a handful of points. Add this as a Phase 0 QA gate, not just length/gap stats.
3. Move primer coverage earlier, or at least compute it in parallel from Phase 1. A region that wins on discriminability but has poor primer coverage for the target clade isn't a usable recommendation, and right
now that correction only lands in Phase 5, after the headline comparisons are already framed.

  One framing note for §11: the tie-in to your SR is real but narrower than the paragraph implies. Your SR's method-dependence finding was 16S-inferred function vs. shotgun-measured function giving different answers.
This study is about region choice driving taxonomic resolution for specific organisms — a related instance of "method choice shapes conclusions in microbiome research," not a test of the same mechanism. I'd word
it as "a further, independent instance of" rather than something that extends or resolves the SR's finding, or a sharp reviewer will ask you to justify a connection you don't need to make.

  Overall: sound design, honest about the likely null, good baselines. The biggest thing between here and a credible submission is making sure a "win" for the gLM isn't just k-mer composition in disguise.


